9. SECURITY NOTICE

9.1 Known Limitations
  - Educational implementation not suitable for production
  - No formal security audit completed
  - Not recommended for real financial data
  - Should be extensively reviewed before any production use
  - Post-quantum cryptography is still being standardized

9.2 Security Considerations
  - Use libOQS for cryptographic operations only
  - Implement additional security measures for production
  - Add comprehensive security testing
  - Conduct formal security audit
  - Implement proper access controls
  - Use secure credential storage
  - Implement transaction signing and verification
  - Add comprehensive logging and monitoring

9.3 Critical Files NOT in Public Repository
  The following sensitive files MUST NEVER be committed to public git:

  Environment Secrets:
  - .env, .env.* files (API keys, secrets)
  - .env.production (production credentials)

  Firebase Configuration (Private):
  - google-services.json (Android)
  - GoogleService-Info.plist (iOS)
  - firebase-adminsdk.json (Service account)

  Keys & Certificates:
  - *.key, *.pem files (RSA/EC private keys)
  - *.p12, *.p8 files (Certificate files)
  - *.jks files (Java keystores)
  - *.keystore files (Android keystores)
  - id_rsa, id_rsa.pub (SSH keys)

  Local Configuration:
  - local.properties (Android SDK path)
  - key.properties (Key store credentials)
  - .gradle/gradle.properties (Private config)

  Build Artifacts:
  - /build/ (Flutter build directory)
  - /android/app/debug, /android/app/profile, /android/app/release
  - .dart_tool/ (Dart compiled files)

  IDE & Editor Files:
  - .vscode/ (IDE configuration)
  - .idea/ (IntelliJ configuration)
  - *.swp, *.swo (Editor swap files)

  System Files:
  - .DS_Store (macOS metadata)
  - .metadata (Flutter metadata)
  - Thumbs.db (Windows thumbnails)

  Sensitive Data:
  - /secrets/ folder (custom secrets)
  - credentials.json (Any credential files)
  - oauth_tokens.json (Authentication tokens)
  - coverage/ (Code coverage data)

  Firestore & Database:
  - firestore-export-* (Database backups)
  - *.backup files (Any backup files)

  Node Modules & Dependencies:
  - /node_modules/ (NPM packages)
  - pubspec.lock (Contains resolved versions - actually tracked!)

  See .gitignore for complete list and reasoning

9.4 .gitignore Best Practices for This Project

  The .gitignore file (committed to repo) specifies what should NOT be tracked:

  Category: Miscellaneous
  - *.class, *.log, *.pyc (Compiled/log files)
  - .swp, .swo, .swn (Editor temp files)
  - .DS_Store (macOS metadata)

  Category: IDE Related
  - .vscode/, .idea/ (IDE configurations)
  - *.iml, *.ipr, *.iws (IntelliJ files)

  Category: Flutter/Dart/Pub
  - /doc/ (Generated documentation)
  - .dart_tool/ (Compiled Dart files)
  - .flutter-plugins, .flutter-plugins-dependencies
  - .metadata (Flutter build metadata)
  - .pub-cache/, .pub/
  - /build/ (Build artifacts)

  Category: Symbolication
  - app.*.symbols (Debug symbols)

  Category: Obfuscation
  - app.*.map.json (Obfuscation maps)

  Category: Android Build
  - /android/app/debug, /android/app/profile, /android/app/release

  Category: Environment & Secrets
  - .env, .env.* (Environment variables)
  - *.key, *.pem (Private keys)
  - *.p12, *.jks (Certificate files)
  - secrets/ (Secrets folder)

  Category: Coverage
  - coverage/ (Code coverage results)
  - *.lcov (Coverage data)

  Category: Generated Code
  - *.g.dart (Generated by build_runner)
  - *.freezed.dart (Generated by freezed)
  - *.mocks.dart (Generated mocks)

  Category: Platform Specific
  - *.dmg, *.app (macOS artifacts)
  - *.exe, *.dll (Windows artifacts)

  Category: Firebase & Configuration
  - firebase_app_id_file.json (Sensitive)
  - key.properties (Android signing)
  - local.properties (Local config)
  - firebase-adminsdk.json (Service account)

  Category: Crashlytics
  - crashlytics-build.properties

  Enforcement Strategy:
  - Use pre-commit hooks to prevent accidental commits
  - Code review process checks for .env files
  - Automated scanning for secrets in CI/CD
  - Regular audits of repository history

  If Secrets Accidentally Committed:
  1. Rotate ALL affected credentials immediately
  2. Remove from git history (git filter-branch or git-filter-repo)
  3. Force push to replace repository history
  4. Update .gitignore to prevent future incidents
  5. Review Git logs for unauthorized access

9.3 Cryptographic Standards
  - Implements NIST PQC standardization recommendations
  - Uses Kyber (ML-KEM-768) for key encapsulation
  - Uses ECDH (secp256r1) for hybrid compatibility
  - Based on published cryptographic research
  - Not cryptographically audited for production

